Any natural number $n > 1$ can be written as a product of primes. Suppose not, and let $C = {n in NN: n > 1 and "n cannot be written as a product of primes"}$. Assuming $C != emptyset$, the well-ordering principle gives a least element $m in C$. Since $m$ is not prime, $m = a times b$ for some $a, b in NN$ with $1 < a, b < m$. By minimality of $m$, both $a$ and $b$ can be written as products of primes, and hence so can $m = a times b$ — a contradiction.
